.TH "vsearch-fastx_syncpairs" "1" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --fastx_syncpairs \[em] reorder paired reads so that mates
appear in the same order in both files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--fastx_syncpairs\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R]
\f[B]--reverse\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R] (\f[B]--fastaout\f[R] |
\f[B]--fastqout\f[R]) \f[I]filename\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--fastx_syncpairs\f[R] reorders paired-end
reads so that the two mates of each pair appear at the same position in
the forward and reverse files.
Forward reads are given as the argument to \f[V]--fastx_syncpairs\f[R],
and the reverse reads are specified with \f[V]--reverse\f[R].
Both files must be in the same format, either fasta or fastq (not a
mix).
See \f[V]vsearch-fasta(5)\f[R] and \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R] for more
information on these formats.
.PP
Two reads form a pair when they share the same \f[I]matching key\f[R].
The key is the read header, truncated at the first whitespace, with a
trailing \f[V]/1\f[R] or \f[V]/2\f[R] mate marker removed (see
\f[V]--read_separators\f[R]).
For example, the Casava 1.8+ headers \f[V]instrument:42 1:N:0:7\f[R] and
\f[V]instrument:42 2:N:0:7\f[R] share the key \f[V]instrument:42\f[R],
and the older headers \f[V]instrument:42/1\f[R] and
\f[V]instrument:42/2\f[R] share the key \f[V]instrument:42\f[R].
.PP
The reverse file is read once and kept in memory; the forward file is
then read entry by entry, and the matching reverse read is retrieved.
Synchronized pairs are written in the order of the forward file: the
forward reads to \f[V]--fastaout\f[R] and/or \f[V]--fastqout\f[R], and
their mates to \f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R] and/or \f[V]--fastqout_rev\f[R].
Neither input file needs to be seekable, so both may be compressed or
read from a pipe.
.PP
A read whose key appears in only one of the two files is called an
\f[I]orphan\f[R].
By default orphans are discarded.
They can instead be written out with \f[V]--fastaout_orphans\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastqout_orphans\f[R] (forward orphans, in forward-file order)
and \f[V]--fastaout_orphans_rev\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastqout_orphans_rev\f[R] (reverse orphans, in reverse-file
order).
.PP
Read labels are expected to be unique within each file.
A duplicated label is reported as an error when it makes the pairing
ambiguous: always for the reverse file, and for the forward file when
two reads claim the same reverse mate.
.PP
Note that reads are only reordered: they are not joined, merged, or
modified.
To join non-overlapping paired-end reads, see
\f[V]vsearch-fastq_join(1)\f[R].
To merge overlapping paired-end reads, see
\f[V]vsearch-fastq_mergepairs(1)\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.TP
\f[V]--reverse\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Specify the fasta or fastq file containing the reverse reads.
The format must match that of the forward reads.
.PP
At least one output file must also be specified.
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the synchronized forward reads to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta
format (see \f[V]vsearch-fasta(5)\f[R]).
Quality scores are not written.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the synchronized reverse reads to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta
format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the synchronized forward reads to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fastq
format (see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
The input must be in fastq format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the synchronized reverse reads to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fastq
format.
The input must be in fastq format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_orphans\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the forward reads that have no reverse mate to \f[I]filename\f[R],
in fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_orphans_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the reverse reads that have no forward mate to \f[I]filename\f[R],
in fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout_orphans\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the forward reads that have no reverse mate to \f[I]filename\f[R],
in fastq format.
The input must be in fastq format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout_orphans_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the reverse reads that have no forward mate to \f[I]filename\f[R],
in fastq format.
The input must be in fastq format.
.TP
\f[V]--read_separators\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Specify the set of characters that, when immediately followed by the
mate number \f[V]1\f[R] or \f[V]2\f[R] at the end of the matching key,
mark a trailing mate marker to be stripped.
The default is \f[V]/\f[R], which removes the \f[V]/1\f[R] and
\f[V]/2\f[R] markers of older Illumina headers.
A whitespace always ends the matching key, regardless of this option, so
Casava 1.8+ headers are handled without any configuration.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_width\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximal width of sequences when writing fasta files.
Longer sequences are folded and written on several lines.
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.SS ignored options
.PP
Reads are reordered without inspecting their quality scores, so the
following quality-related options are accepted (for instance in a
pipeline) but have no effect.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] 33|64
Option is ignored and has no effect.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Option is ignored: quality scores are not checked when reading fastq
sequences, so no score is rejected for being too high.
The argument itself is still validated, as the offset (see
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the maximal score may not exceed 126, the
last printable ASCII character.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Option is ignored: quality scores are not checked when reading fastq
sequences, so no score is rejected for being too low.
The argument itself is still validated, as the offset (see
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the minimal score must be at least 33, the
first printable ASCII character.
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Command is not multithreaded, option has no effect (a warning is printed
when more than one thread is requested).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Reorder the reads of \f[I]R1.fastq\f[R] and \f[I]R2.fastq\f[R] so that
mates line up, writing the synchronized reads to
\f[I]R1_sorted.fastq\f[R] and \f[I]R2_sorted.fastq\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastx_syncpairs R1.fastq \[rs]
    --reverse R2.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout R1_sorted.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_rev R2_sorted.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Same as above, but also keep the unpaired reads in separate files:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastx_syncpairs R1.fastq \[rs]
    --reverse R2.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout R1_sorted.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_rev R2_sorted.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_orphans R1_orphans.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_orphans_rev R2_orphans.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Synchronize reads whose headers carry the mate number after an
underscore (for example \f[V]readid_1\f[R] and \f[V]readid_2\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastx_syncpairs R1.fastq \[rs]
    --reverse R2.fastq \[rs]
    --read_separators _ \[rs]
    --fastqout R1_sorted.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_rev R2_sorted.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\f[V]vsearch-fastq_join(1)\f[R] for joining non-overlapping paired-end
reads, \f[V]vsearch-fastq_mergepairs(1)\f[R] for merging overlapping
paired-end reads.
\f[V]vsearch-fasta(5)\f[R], \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]
